// §5 "Worth knowing". Same job as the hub's Award block and the carpet
// sibling's warranty block: make the reader distrust the cheapest quote
// without making a single claim about TLB. Here the lever is that pressure
// cleaning is the one trade where the cheap operator can do permanent damage
// in about four seconds, and the second is the legal one.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFY BEFORE LAUNCH. The stormwater point is a statement about the law
// and it is correct in substance for NSW: under the Protection of the
// Environment Operations Act 1997 (NSW), allowing polluting matter to enter
// stormwater is an offence, and councils and the EPA both enforce it. It is
// worded here as a duty on the site rather than as legal advice, and it names
// no section number and no penalty amount on purpose, because those change.
// Have someone confirm the phrasing, and consider linking NSW EPA or the
// relevant council directly (BFD 3.3 asks for authoritative sources).
const worthKnowingBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Pressure cleaning is the one trade where a cheap operator can do permanent damage in about four seconds. Too much pressure held too long etches concrete into a visible stripe, cuts the sand out from between pavers, strips paint off steel, furs up timber decking and drives water in behind cladding, where it goes on causing damage after the machine has gone. None of that shows in a quote. It shows on your building, and it does not wash off.',
  'The second thing worth knowing is that the water has to go somewhere. Run-off carrying oil, grease, detergent or food waste must not enter a stormwater drain, because stormwater goes straight to the creek and then the ocean without treatment, and the site occupier can be the one held responsible. So ask any cleaner what they do about wash water before you let them start, especially around a car park, a loading dock or a bin bay. It is a fair question and a competent operator will have a straight answer.',
];

