//     your council" is doing real work in that answer, not hedging.
//   - Slip hazard and exclusion zones. A genuine WHS duty on the site
//     occupier under the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (NSW), phrased here
//     without naming it.
//   - Frequency ("twice a year for most sites") is an industry observation,
//     not a rule, and is hedged accordingly.
// Recommended before launch: a read by someone who prices exterior work, and
// a visible "last reviewed" date on this block.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How often should a commercial site be pressure cleaned?',
    answer:
      'Most sites settle on twice a year, with high traffic entries, bin bays and food areas done quarterly. In the Northern Rivers the shaded southern side of a building grows algae far faster than the sunny side, so it is common for one elevation to need doing twice as often as the rest. Surfaces kept on a schedule cost less each visit, because there is less built up to remove.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between pressure cleaning and soft washing?',
    answer:
      'Pressure cleaning uses force to remove what is sitting on a hard surface, and suits concrete, pavers, brick and steel. Soft washing uses low pressure and a cleaning solution, and suits render, paint, cladding, signage and roofs, where high pressure would strip the finish or force water behind it. Soft washing also treats the algae and mould at the root, so the surface stays clean longer.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can pressure cleaning damage concrete?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Concrete looks tough but it etches. Holding the wand too close, or too long in one spot, cuts a visible line into the surface that does not fade, and a rotary surface cleaner is used on large flat areas partly to avoid exactly that. Old concrete, decorative finishes, sandstone and aged pavers are the ones to be careful with, and any of them should be tested in a corner first.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Where is the water allowed to go?',
    answer:
      'Clean rinse water off a footpath can usually go where rain would. Water carrying oil, grease, detergent, paint or food waste cannot: in NSW, letting polluting matter enter a stormwater drain is an offence under the Protection of the Environment Operations Act 1997, and stormwater runs to the creek untreated. That run-off has to be captured and disposed of to sewer or to a licensed facility, and discharge to sewer needs your water authority or council to agree the conditions. Ask any cleaner how they handle it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does commercial pressure cleaning cost?',
    answer:
